course: dictionary
lesson: 5175
title: Israeli: a word about people and places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Israeli» is an adjective when it describes something: «Israeli food» is food from Israel, and «the Israeli government» is the government of Israel. It is a noun when it names a person: «an Israeli» is one person from Israel, and «Israelis» means more than one.
explain: Be careful not to mix it up with «Israelite». An Israelite was a person of ancient Israel, in the Bible, a very long time ago. The two words look alike, but they do not mean the same thing.
explain: News reports often name two peoples, Israeli and Palestinian. «You can feel for Israeli and Palestinian families at the same time.» Good readers think about both peoples and what they go through, not just one side.
fact: Israeli can be an adjective (describing a thing) or a noun (naming a person).
fact: «Israeli» means from or belonging to the modern country of Israel, which was founded in 1948.
fact: «Israelite» is a different word: it means a person of ancient Israel, in the Bible.
fact: The plural of «an Israeli» is «Israelis».
fact: Some Israeli and Palestinian families who lost children meet to talk together, and talks about peace are still going on.
exercise: In «Israeli food», what does Israeli describe? | A person who cooks food | *Food from or belonging to Israel | Food from ancient Israel in the Bible | Food that is very spicy
exercise: «Many Israeli families had to leave their homes for months, news reports said.» Which word tells you where the families are from? | homes | families | months | *Israeli
exercise: What is the difference between «Israeli» and «Israelite»? | An Israeli lived in the Bible long ago | An Israelite is a modern word for a type of food | *An Israeli is from modern Israel; an Israelite was a person of ancient Israel in the Bible | They mean exactly the same thing
test: What is the plural of «an Israeli»? | Israelish | Israelies | Israels | *Israelis
test: Which two peoples does the news often name as the two sides in the conflict? | Jordanian and Egyptian | *Israeli and Palestinian | Greek and Turkish | Israelite and Roman
exam: «You can feel for Israeli and Palestinian families at the same time.» Why is it good to think about both peoples? | It is a sum about families | *It shows care for families on both sides | It means the two words are the same | It means only one side is right
